## 1. The methods, in plain words

- **Walk-forward validation.** Each month from {{ vy[0] }} to {{ vy[1] }} is forecast by a model refitted on the hours before it only, minus the last {{ K.embargo_days }} days (the embargo). Nothing from the tested month helps fit it.
- **The error.** Mean absolute percentage error (MAPE): on average, how far the forecast is from actual demand. The signed bias says in which direction: bias = (demand − forecast) / demand, positive when the forecast is too low.
- **The uncertainty of a mean.** Neighbouring hours share the weather, so whole weeks are resampled, {{ K.n_boot|num }} times, to get a 95% interval around each mean. "A beats B" is written only when the interval of the difference excludes zero.
- **The prediction interval.** Its half-width is a quantile of the relative errors |demand − forecast| / forecast. For each validation month, it is the quantile of rank ⌈(n + 1) × {{ nominal|num(2) }}⌉ of the n out-of-sample errors of the previous {{ K.calibration_folds }} months that were already scored when the month's model is fitted (section 8), each of those months forecast by its own refitted model; for the frozen model, the same quantile over the errors of the {{ K.calibration_folds }} months before the replayed year that were already scored when it is fitted, applied to a model that made none of those errors. That is the shape of a split-conformal interval, but its finite-sample coverage guarantee does not hold here: it assumes exchangeable errors, and these hourly errors are autocorrelated and come from other models. The only evidence is the coverage measured out of sample (sections 4, 6 and 7).
- **The frozen model.** In production the model is not refitted: it is fitted once, frozen with the hash of its coefficients, then replayed hour by hour.
- **The thresholds.** For each season, the weekly thresholds are quantiles of the validation weeks: MAPE above the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.mape_high) }} percentile, bias outside the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.bias_low) }}–{{ ordinal(K.centiles.bias_high) }} percentiles, coverage below the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.coverage_low) }} percentile.
- **Drift.** Each week, the distribution of temperatures and of forecasts is compared with the same weeks (± {{ K.drift_half_window }}) of earlier years, using Evidently's normed Wasserstein distance. The alarm level is the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.drift_high) }} percentile of that distance over the validation weeks.
- **The decision table.** Each week, at most one recommendation, with its evidence: (1) a data-quality failure → investigate the data; (2) the same failure {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks running (MAPE above its threshold, or bias beyond the same side of its band), broad across each week, or the bias beyond the same side of its band {{ K.sustained_weeks }} weeks running → retrain; (3) coverage below its threshold {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks running, broad, with bias inside its band → recalibrate the intervals; (4) the same input drifting {{ K.consecutive_weeks }} weeks running, with performance inside its thresholds → investigate the data. "Broad": the week stays beyond the threshold when its {{ K.trim_days }} most extreme days are set aside. One bad day never touches more than two days of a week (itself and, through the {{ K.lags[0] }}-hour and {{ K.lags[1] }}-hour lags, the next day or the same day a week later), so it cannot trigger a recommendation unless the weeks already held failing days of their own. A bad Monday-to-Sunday week comes back the next week, through the week-old lag, with the opposite sign: not the same failure twice, so with its neighbouring weeks inside their bands it triggers nothing either. An episode of {{ K.trim_days + 1 }} days or more can trigger "retrain" on its own; section 6 measures how often. The monitor recommends; it never acts.

