{% set DY = drift_years %}
- **False drift alarms in {{ r.year }}.** The alarm levels are the {{ ordinal(K.centiles.drift_high) }} percentile of the {{ span(K.threshold_years[r.year|string]) }} validation weeks, whose references held {{ DY.temperature.calibration[0] }} to {{ DY.temperature.calibration[1] }} earlier years for temperature and {{ DY.forecast.calibration[0] }} to {{ DY.forecast.calibration[1] }} for the forecast; in {{ r.year }}, they hold {{ DY.temperature.replay }} and {{ DY.forecast.replay }}. The false drift-alarm rate in {{ r.year }} is not measured.
- **Rule 4 and a real change in temperature.** A real, sustained change in temperature, which demand responds to and the model follows, with performance inside its thresholds, can yield "investigate the data": the rule cannot tell a real change in the weather from a data error (break "{{ (b.breaks|selectattr('test', 'equalto', 'tests/test_replay.py::test_temperature_drift_yields_investigate_with_performance_inside')|first).en }}", section 9). The rule stays as written.
- **Data quality in {{ r.year }}.** The replay judges each day on the raw records cut at the clock, but it runs only if the whole window, {{ r.year }} included, passed the data-quality report's gates. Out-of-bounds values, off-grid records and duplicated temperatures therefore could not appear in the replay; the daily monitor still judged missing hours, blank values, duplicated demand records and a silent primary station there.
- **Real-time data.** Values are as downloaded on {{ dq.sources.hydroquebec.retrieved_utc[:10] }} (demand) and from {{ (dq.sources.eccc|map(attribute='first_retrieved_utc')|min)[:10] }} on (weather); no publication delay and no revision are modelled. Design code sees each decision time as the replay sees its clock: the demand series is rebuilt there from only the raw records stamped at or before that time (for a model's fit, the origin of its first test day; for the lags, each day's origin; for the target, the time the day is scored).{% for e in dq.checks.demand_misplaced.evidence %} The repair that fills the missing hour of {{ e.moved_to_utc|utc }} with a record stamped {{ e.duplicated_utc|utc }} therefore enters design code only after that record arrives, at the same time as in the replays.{% endfor %} An interval's half-width, fixed when its model is fitted, is calibrated only on the days already scored at that time (a day is scored an hour after its last hour, or at the end of the design data): the day before a validation month's first test day stays out.{{ '' }}
- **Independence.** The model, the checks and this report come from one AI agent session under one owner's direction, with no independent human review of the code. The reviews were done by fresh-context agents, which is not independent validation in the SR 11-7 sense.

## 9. Breaks, on synthetic data

Each gate and monitor has a test that injects the fault it exists to catch; a control injects nothing and must stay quiet. The `breaks` run executes them all; the report refuses to render if any of them failed. No break reads the real demand or weather data: the data-quality gates, the guards and the rendering are tested on small made-up data, and the clock and the monitors in a synthetic world (`tests/replay_world.py`): just over two years of made-up demand, a model frozen on it, thresholds drawn from its own out-of-sample weeks by the same quantile rule as the real ones (one set for all four seasons), and a drift alarm level set by hand at {{ world.drift_alarm|num(1) }}, not by that rule. A break firing in that world shows the check can fire, not that it would see the same fault in real demand.

